    <sectiondef kind="func">
      <memberdef kind="function" id="namespacethemis_1_1observability_1_1detail_1a0e9c105da5f81890e6b60120e006f151" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="yes" noexcept="yes" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>bool</type>
        <definition>bool themis::observability::detail::gmtime_utc</definition>
        <argsstring>(std::time_t tt, std::tm &amp;result) noexcept</argsstring>
        <name>gmtime_utc</name>
        <qualifiedname>themis::observability::detail::gmtime_utc</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>std::time_t</type>
          <declname>tt</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>std::tm &amp;</type>
          <declname>result</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Thread-safe UTC breakdown of a <computeroutput>time_t</computeroutput> value. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para><computeroutput>std::gmtime()</computeroutput> returns a pointer to static storage that is not protected by any lock, making it unsafe to call concurrently. This wrapper uses the POSIX <computeroutput>gmtime_r()</computeroutput> on non-Windows platforms, and the Windows-specific <computeroutput>gmtime_s()</computeroutput> on MSVC/MinGW.</para>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>tt</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Input POSIX timestamp. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>result</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Output <computeroutput>std::tm</computeroutput> structure filled in UTC. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para><computeroutput>true</computeroutput> on success; <computeroutput>false</computeroutput> if the platform call fails. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
        <location file="include/utils/field_diagnostics_schema.h" line="45" column="13" bodyfile="include/utils/field_diagnostics_schema.h" bodystart="45" bodyend="51"/>
      </memberdef>
      <memberdef kind="function" id="namespacethemis_1_1observability_1_1detail_1a9b3a2fa88bd570718f02d3a99913b74c" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="yes" noexcept="yes" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>std::time_t</type>
        <definition>std::time_t themis::observability::detail::mktime_utc</definition>
        <argsstring>(std::tm &amp;tm) noexcept</argsstring>
        <name>mktime_utc</name>
        <qualifiedname>themis::observability::detail::mktime_utc</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>std::tm &amp;</type>
          <declname>tm</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Inverse of <computeroutput><ref refid="namespacethemis_1_1observability_1_1detail_1a0e9c105da5f81890e6b60120e006f151" kindref="member">gmtime_utc()</ref></computeroutput>: convert a UTC <computeroutput>std::tm</computeroutput> to <computeroutput>time_t</computeroutput>. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para><computeroutput>std::mktime()</computeroutput> interprets its argument as <emphasis>local</emphasis> time, which produces incorrect results for UTC timestamps when the host timezone differs from UTC. This wrapper uses POSIX <computeroutput>timegm()</computeroutput> on non-Windows platforms and the Windows-specific <computeroutput>_mkgmtime()</computeroutput> on MSVC/MinGW.</para>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>tm</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>UTC-based <computeroutput>std::tm</computeroutput> structure. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>Corresponding POSIX <computeroutput>time_t</computeroutput>, or <computeroutput>(time_t)-1</computeroutput> on failure. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
